#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/gui-troubleshooting.rst:3
#: 8e5bfabbb727479a8eaf645753201e55
msgid "GUI troubleshooting"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/gui-troubleshooting.rst:7
#: 4b79285a215b42aaa4fec16757551bd0
msgid ""
"Can't click on parts of the screen after connecting high-resolution external "
"display"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/gui-troubleshooting.rst:10
#: cb0ab6f7629549bb804ca87e7fb4e503
msgid ""
"When you connect a high-resolution external display, you may be unable to "
"click on parts of the screen."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/gui-troubleshooting.rst:12
#: f9c9814f48944743a48eae8e60b270e2
msgid ""
"When a qube starts, a fixed amount of RAM is allocated to the graphics "
"buffer called video RAM. This buffer needs to be at least as big as the "
"whole desktop, accounting for all displays that are or will be connected to "
"the machine. By default, it is as much as needed for the current display and "
"an additional full HD (FHD) display (1920×1080 8 bit/channel RGBA). This "
"logic fails when the machine has primary display in FHD resolution and, "
"after starting some qubes, a high-resolution display is connected. If the "
"buffer is too small, and internal desktop resize fails."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/gui-troubleshooting.rst:14
#: f5dea794b16f4a348db5142939b6f5e6
msgid ""
"To determine if this is the problem affecting you, look at the Xorg log "
"inside the Qube at ``/home/user/.local/share/xorg/Xorg.0.log`` for lines "
"like the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/gui-troubleshooting.rst:22
#: bd0934f8d11b47f59ba54ce952e9dfa6
msgid ""
"The solution to this problem is to increase the minimum size of the video "
"RAM buffer, as explained in :ref:`GUI Configuration <user/advanced-topics/"
"gui-configuration:video ram adjustment for high-resolution displays>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/gui-troubleshooting.rst:25
#: 769649b090ca41e5b36586ef44d5d1e8
msgid ""
"Screen blanks / Weird computer glitches like turning partially black or "
"black boxes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/gui-troubleshooting.rst:28
#: 93a832dc6e1343f3a874efd86bb0f282
msgid ""
"You may encountering seemingly random screen blanking while using Qubes, "
"where the screen will black and shows the logon screen, yet, only the active "
"window will show when you move the mouse or use the keyboard. Sometimes, you "
"will get random black screens or black boxes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/gui-troubleshooting.rst:30
#: bd9dbe2d122742dcac9e14115cec33fb
msgid ""
"Similarly, while working, the XScreenSaver dialog may pop up (indicating the "
"screen is locked) and the screen goes black. However, the screen is not "
"locked, and you have to move a window to redraw the screen."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/gui-troubleshooting.rst:32
#: 0410037d4c7a4b65bd97f5ba96338fce
msgid ""
"If you are experiencing the any of the above symptoms, try disabling the "
"window compositor:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/gui-troubleshooting.rst:34
#: 9a48b8b00a974ab7a0a910f2efed1ec2
msgid ""
"``Q → System Tools → Window Manager Tweaks → Compositor → uncheck “Enable "
"display compositing”``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/gui-troubleshooting.rst:37
#: c47e9e34a0664b62b033a4717b249626
msgid ""
"Post installation, screen goes black and freezes following LUKS decryption"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/gui-troubleshooting.rst:40
#: d9527de2177c46459183ce57c9c33442
msgid ""
"After installing Qubes, you may experience a black screen after entering "
"your LUKS decryption password. To fix the problem, use your preferred text "
"editor (``nano`` works) to edit ``/mnt/sysimage/boot/efi/EFI/qubes/xen."
"cfg``, adding the ``efi=no-rs`` option to the end of the ``options= line``. "
"For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/gui-troubleshooting.rst:49
#: 46fc7c899de84ccfb618d593bba30a67
msgid ""
"Note that the ``/mnt/sysimage/boot/efi/EFI/qubes/xen.cfg`` path applies when "
"running from the installer (either directly after installation, before the "
"reboot, or by starting the installer again in recovery mode). On the actual "
"installed system, the file to edit is ``/boot/efi/EFI/qubes/xen.cfg`` – but "
"it may be hard to access directly when your system won’t boot."
msgstr ""

